    {
      "id": "08-pros",
      "index": 7,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "pros",
      "text": "Positives first: works as a savoury meal anchor. They slot into bowls, soups, tacos, and rice plates without needing much. Cheap shelf-stable nutrition. Dry black beans are easy to store and scale, which matters for real diets. Dark seed coat adds a polyphenol angle. The black seed coat gives them a more specific plant-compound story than plain starch.",
      "textSha256": "179e10b5069e7d07e6f25581a437fc472879163475938c7ff3775c5df60f0ca8",
      "characterCount": 354,
      "byteLength": 345278,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/black-beans/voice-v2-blocks/08-pros.mp3",
      "elevenLabs": {
        "requestId": "VMTXoej8lACZ5qEv6peM",
        "historyItemId": "ivewDebPy2Dbw1a92mhM"
      },
      "productionAudioFile": "production/episodes/black-beans/voice/voice-v2-blocks/08-pros.mp3"
    },
    {
      "id": "09-cons",
      "index": 8,
      "kind": "section",
      "sectionKey": "cons",
      "text": "Cons next: takes planning to cook from dry. The strongest version is not instant food. Digestive tolerance varies. The same fibre and oligosaccharides that help the score can bother some people. Canned versions can add sodium. The plain dry record is clean, but common convenience versions can change the downside.",
      "textSha256": "ce41092d188cc3240122a18df27fe79489aad60ac0bb60cee1863b114b6b4913",
      "characterCount": 314,
      "byteLength": 340680,
      "audioFile": "docs/audio/episodes/black-beans/voice-v2-blocks/09-cons.mp3",
      "elevenLabs": {
